Keep your pipeline on GitHub Actions and connect it to Nx Cloud for remote caching, workers, and self-healing CI.
To run the whole pipeline on Nx Cloud instead, see [setting up CI](/docs/getting-started/setup-ci).
For GitLab, CircleCI, Azure Pipelines, Bitbucket Pipelines, and Jenkins, see [other CI providers](#other-ci-providers).

## Connect your workspace

Push your workspace to a remote repository, then run:

```shell
npx nx@latest connect
```

Remote caching works as soon as the workspace is connected, with no configuration file.
For read-only and read-write CI tokens, see [CI access tokens](/docs/kb/access-tokens).

## Run CI with Nx

Caching, affected, and distribution only apply to tasks that `nx` runs.
`nx test` is fine, and so is `npm test` if it wraps `nx test`.
Direct calls to `jest`, `tsc`, or `eslint` skip Nx.

Use `nx affected` to run tasks only for projects your change touches:

```yaml
# .github/workflows/ci.yml
# ... name and triggers
jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v7
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0
      # ... set up Node.js
      - run: npm ci
      - uses: nrwl/nx-set-shas@v5
      - run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
```

`fetch-depth: 0` gives Nx the git history it needs to compare commits.
On a pull request, Nx compares the branch with `main` (or `defaultBase` in `nx.json`).
On a push to `main`, `nrwl/nx-set-shas` sets the base to the last commit that passed CI.

If you don't have a CI workflow yet, generate one:

```shell
nx add @nx/workspace
nx g @nx/workspace:ci-workflow --ci=github
```

Supported `--ci` values: `github`, `circleci`, `gitlab`, `azure`, `bitbucket-pipelines`.
The generated workflow also includes the Nx Cloud steps described below.

## Distribute tasks with workers

Set the number of workers in `.nx/pipelines/ci.yaml`:

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
worker-templates:
  default: 3 linux-large-js
```

`linux-large-js` is a predefined [launch template](/docs/kb/launch-templates).
If the generator created `.nx/ci-config.yaml` instead, as older Nx versions do, [migrate it](/docs/kb/migrate-from-ci-config) first, because `start-workers` keeps reading that file while it exists.

Then start the workers early in your CI job, before installing dependencies, so they boot while the job installs:

```yaml {% meta="{7}" %}
# .github/workflows/ci.yml
jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      # ... checkout
      - run: npx nx-cloud start-workers
      # ... set up Node.js, install dependencies, and set SHAs
      - run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
```

Every Nx command in the job then runs its tasks across the three workers.
For how Nx Cloud fills each worker, see [distribute task execution](/docs/features/ci-features/distribute-task-execution).
For every configuration option, see the [CI configuration file reference](/docs/reference/nx-cloud/ci-config).

## Add self-healing CI

On your own CI provider, add a step that runs `nx fix-ci` at the end of the job that runs `nx-cloud start-workers` and your `nx affected` or `nx run-many` commands.
The step must run even when earlier steps fail, so use `if: always()` or your provider's equivalent.
When every task succeeds, `nx fix-ci` does nothing.

{% tabs syncKey="ci-provider" %}

{% tabitem label="GitHub" %}

```yaml
# .github/workflows/ci.yml
jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      # ... checkout, start-workers, set up Node.js
      - run: npm ci
      - run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
      - run: npx nx fix-ci
        if: always()
```

{% /tabitem %}

{% tabitem label="GitLab" %}

```yaml
# .gitlab-ci.yml
main:
  stage: build
  script:
    - npx nx-cloud start-workers
    - npm ci
    - npx nx affected -t lint test build
  # after_script runs whether the job succeeds or fails
  after_script:
    - npx nx fix-ci
```

{% /tabitem %}

{% tabitem label="Azure" %}

```yaml
# azure-pipelines.yml
steps:
  # ... set up Node.js
  - script: npx nx-cloud start-workers
  - script: npm ci
  - script: npx nx affected -t lint test build
  - script: npx nx fix-ci
    condition: always()
```

{% /tabitem %}

{% tabitem label="Bitbucket" %}

```yaml
# bitbucket-pipelines.yml
pipelines:
  pull-requests:
    '**':
      - step:
          name: CI
          script:
            - npx nx-cloud start-workers
            - npm ci
            - npx nx affected -t lint test build
          # after-script runs whether the step succeeds or fails
          after-script:
            - npx nx fix-ci
```

{% /tabitem %}

{% /tabs %}

Self-healing CI also needs a [VCS integration](/docs/kb/source-control-integration) and the setting turned on in your workspace.
For both, see [self-healing CI](/docs/features/ci-features/self-healing-ci).

## Complete example

This pipeline combines affected, remote caching, distribution, and self-healing CI:

```yaml
// .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
worker-templates:
  default:
    # Distribute across 3 workers using the linux-large-js launch template
    workers: 3 linux-large-js
    # Shut idle workers down once every build task has been requested
    stop-after:
      - build
```

```yaml
// .github/workflows/ci.yml
name: CI

on:
  push:
    branches:
      - main
  pull_request:

permissions:
  actions: read
  contents: read

jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      # fetch-depth: 0 gives Nx the full history it needs to work out what changed
      - uses: actions/checkout@v7
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0
          filter: tree:0

      # Provisions the workers declared in .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml.
      # Run it before installing dependencies so workers boot while this job installs.
      - run: npx nx-cloud start-workers

      - uses: actions/setup-node@v6
        with:
          node-version: 24
          cache: 'npm'

      - run: npm ci

      # Sets the base to the last commit that passed CI on main
      - uses: nrwl/nx-set-shas@v5

      # Runs lint, test, and build only for projects affected by this change.
      # Nx Cloud distributes these tasks across the workers started above.
      - run: npx nx affected -t lint test build

      - run: npx nx fix-ci
        if: always()
```

## Other CI providers

On other providers, the steps are the same, but the pipeline syntax differs:

- [CI workflow examples](/docs/kb/ci-workflow-examples) has a complete pipeline for CircleCI, GitLab, Azure Pipelines, Jenkins, and Bitbucket Pipelines.
- [Set the affected base commit in CI](/docs/kb/affected-base-commit) covers how each provider passes `NX_BASE` and `NX_HEAD`.
- For self-healing CI, use the GitLab, Azure, or Bitbucket tab in [add self-healing CI](#add-self-healing-ci).

To see Nx Cloud run results on each merge or pull request, connect [GitLab](/docs/guides/nx-cloud/source-control-integration/gitlab), [Bitbucket](/docs/guides/nx-cloud/source-control-integration/bitbucket), or [Azure DevOps](/docs/guides/nx-cloud/source-control-integration/azure-devops).

